Recompiler and runtime: vmax and vmini order the bit patterns as signed integers, like the console.

What it fixes: the emitted text was _mm_max_ps / _mm_min_ps, so -0 and +0 compared equal (the second operand won) and a denormal
pattern was a zero. The reference emulator (third_party/pcsx2/pcsx2/VUops.cpp applyMinMax, with fp_max / fp_min of FPU.cpp)
compares the two patterns as signed integers, two negative ones the other way round: max(+0, -0) is +0, min is -0 and a
denormal pattern is a larger number than zero. Two helpers in the runtime header do that per lane (ps2_vu_max_ps, ps2_vu_min_ps);
the emitted text calls them. Same rule as patch 0049 for max.s and min.s.

How it was verified: tests/conformance family "vu0", vmax, vmaxx..w, vmaxi, vmini, vminix..w, vminii: 235 to 416 of 546 to 1,157
cases differed before, none after.
Sites in the game: vmax 87 (mask 0x3), vmini 87 (mask 0x3), vmaxx 1, vminix 1. The difference needs a signed zero or a denormal
as an operand.

Original work for this repository (no code from another project).

